<Article slug="stampedes">
<p>A popular product card expires. Twelve visitors ask for it before the first replacement has finished loading. Each request looks in Redis, finds nothing and starts the same database query.</p>
<p>The cache needed one answer. The source receives twelve jobs.</p>
<p>This is a <strong>cache stampede</strong>, also called a <strong>thundering herd</strong>. Expiry is one trigger; eviction, invalidation, a new deployment and a cold cache can produce the same overlap. A fast miss does not tell a caller whether somebody else is already rebuilding the answer.</p>
<p>Play the burst below, then replay it with coordination within each process and across all processes. The requests stay the same; the amount of source work changes.</p>
<StampedeBurst />
<p>Without coordination, four loads execute while eight queue. The last callers wait for the third wave. With one load per application process, three loads serve all twelve callers. Sharing one load across the fleet reduces the source work further, but requires coordination beyond a process’s own memory.</p>

<h2>Request coalescing and singleflight</h2>
<p><strong>Request coalescing</strong> lets callers needing the same result share one operation already in progress. One caller starts the load; the others wait for its result. This is also known as <strong>singleflight</strong>, the name used by Go’s duplicate-call-suppression package.</p>
<p>The shared operation is temporary. A cache reuses an answer after it has been computed; coalescing reuses the work while it is being computed. They complement each other. A useful loading path checks the cache, joins or starts a flight for the key, then has the chosen loader check the cache again before reading the source. Another request may have filled it between the first check and becoming the loader.</p>
<p>The flight key needs the same answer-identity discipline as the cache key. Sharing loads across tenants, languages or permission contexts that need different answers is just another way to return the wrong result. Coalescing is safe only for callers allowed to receive the same outcome.</p>
<p>A local implementation might keep a map from key to an in-progress promise. It must remove completed and failed work, and it must bound the number of distinct in-flight keys and waiting callers. Otherwise an optimisation for the database can become an unbounded memory queue in the application.</p>
<p>Cancellation needs care too. One caller abandoning its request should not necessarily cancel a load still needed by other callers. Conversely, a shared load should not run forever merely because somebody might still want it. Define a load deadline separately from each caller’s waiting deadline, and decide how cancellation reaches the source client.</p>
<p>In the illustration, increase the process count while using local coordination. Each process has its own map, so six processes can start six loads. Local singleflight can remove a large amount of duplication without adding a distributed protocol, but its scope must be understood. Adding application instances can increase the number of simultaneous source loads even when every instance is individually well behaved.</p>
<p>The exposure window is the time until a usable fill becomes visible. At a roughly steady arrival rate of 1,000 requests per second, a 200 ms load leaves room for about 200 arrivals during that window. This is an illustrative rate-times-duration estimate, not a workload guarantee. If duplicate work slows the source, the window grows and admits still more requests: a feedback loop.</p>

<h2>Redis leases coordinate a shared loader</h2>
<p>To coordinate across processes, applications sometimes use a short-lived Redis key as a <strong>lease</strong>: temporary permission to perform a cache fill. A contender tries to create the lease only if it does not already exist:</p>
<pre><code>{`SET fill:{product-42} unique-owner-token NX PX 2000`}</code></pre>
<p><code>NX</code> requires the key to be absent; <code>PX</code> gives the lease a lifetime in milliseconds. An <code>OK</code> reply means this attempt acquired it. A nil reply means it did not. The two-second lifetime here is an example, not a recommendation. The token must identify this particular acquisition, not merely the process or hostname.</p>
<p>The owner rechecks the value cache, performs any necessary source work, then fills and releases. Other callers follow a bounded waiting policy: perhaps wait briefly with jitter, recheck the value, and stop when their deadline is exhausted. If every loser immediately falls back to the source, the lease has not suppressed the stampede.</p>
<p>A lease has a useful failure property: a crashed owner cannot hold it forever. Its deadline also creates a new problem. The owner might be paused or slow rather than dead. Expiring its Redis key does not stop its database query.</p>
<CacheLease />
<p>At B’s acquisition, A is still loading. There are two source operations even though Redis has only one current lease owner. This is why a cache-fill lease does not guarantee exactly one execution.</p>
<p>When A eventually finishes, it must not unconditionally delete the lease. That key now belongs to B. Compare the stored token with A’s token and delete only if they match, in one atomic Redis operation. A separate token read followed by <code>DEL</code> leaves a race between the two commands.</p>
<p>The same principle can protect the cache fill. A short Lua script can check ownership, save the value and release the lease atomically. A late owner whose token no longer matches cannot use that operation. Disable A’s check in the illustration: it writes its old result and removes B’s lease, preventing the careful successor from filling.</p>
<p>Ownership is still not source freshness. An owner can read data that later changes while its lease remains valid. The invalidation-generation protocol in the previous article addresses a different boundary. If both checks are required, combine them into a coherent fill operation rather than assuming one kind of token substitutes for the other.</p>
<p>Lease expiry, eviction and failover can all undermine an assumption that only one worker is active. This pattern is useful for reducing duplicate cache work that the system can tolerate. Do not promote it into the sole correctness mechanism for an irreversible source action. Fencing and stronger coordination requirements belong to a different contract.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/stampedes.sh" download>Redis cache-lease worksheet</a> demonstrates acquisition, expiry, rejection of a late owner, atomic fill/release and the unsafe unconditional deletion. It uses Lua compatible with Redis 8.2.2. Save it and run <code>sh stampedes.sh</code> with Docker available; it creates and removes its own network-isolated container. The assertions were executed during preparation. Source operations are not executed or cancelled by the worksheet.</p>

<h2>TTL jitter spreads many keys’ expirations</h2>
<p>Coalescing limits duplicate work for one answer. A cache can also lose many different answers together. Suppose a startup routine loads sixty keys and gives every one the same sixty-second TTL. Their deadlines line up, creating the conditions for a burst of misses across the whole set.</p>
<p><strong>TTL jitter</strong> varies the deadlines. If sixty seconds is the maximum allowed lifetime, we can shorten some entries by a random amount rather than extending others beyond the freshness budget.</p>
<ExpiryJitter />
<p>At zero jitter, all sixty keys share one deadline. With a twenty-second spread, the deadlines fall between forty and sixty seconds. The largest group is smaller in this sample. The cache still contains the same sixty keys initially, and none receives permission to live longer than sixty seconds.</p>
<p>This does not turn expiry into automatic refreshing. It distributes when keys become unavailable; source work happens when requests subsequently need them, or when an explicit refresh mechanism runs. If traffic arrives in its own synchronised burst, the resulting load can still be concentrated.</p>
<p>Nor does jitter solve the opening example. Twelve callers missing the same key still agree that it is absent. That key has one deadline, however it was chosen. Use coalescing for duplicate work on one key and jitter for correlated timing across many keys.</p>
<p>Shortening lifetimes spends some reuse to reduce synchronisation. Measure that trade-off. Random choices can also cluster by chance; the histogram is a reproducible example, not a guarantee of a perfectly flat workload. A periodic “refresh everything now” task can undo the spread you carefully introduced.</p>

<h2>Stale-while-revalidate separates refreshing from serving</h2>
<p>So far, a reader waits whenever the useful cached answer disappears. Sometimes the application can serve the existing answer for a short, explicit interval while one worker refreshes it.</p>
<p><strong>Stale-while-revalidate</strong> separates two deadlines. A soft threshold says when a saved answer should be refreshed. A hard limit says when the application must stop serving it. Between them, callers may receive the old answer promptly while a coalesced refresh runs in the background.</p>
<p>The term is established in HTTP caching. Here we apply the idea in application code around Redis; Redis does not interpret HTTP cache-control headers. The application must store and enforce the age metadata, keep the value available through the permitted stale window, and coordinate refreshes.</p>
<StaleRefresh />
<p>Advance to ten seconds and read twice. Both requests receive £12, within the stated policy, while only one refresh runs. Finish it and the saved answer becomes £15. The fresh period hides neither source changes nor their consequences: serving the older price is an intentional part of the contract.</p>
<p>Reset and fail the refresh instead. Its failure does not reset the old observation time. At twenty seconds the old copy is too old to serve. A read must wait within a deadline for an acceptable result, use an authorised fallback within its capacity budget, or fail in a controlled way. Press End waiting request to see that one caller’s timeout need not stop the shared source operation.</p>
<p>The model includes a short retry backoff after failure. Without one, successive requests could each start a failing refresh as soon as the previous attempt ends. Singleflight bounds simultaneous duplication; it does not by itself bound the rate of sequential failures.</p>
<p>Loading time also counts. Start a refresh at ten seconds, advance time and then finish it. The new value retains its observation time of ten seconds. It does not acquire a brand-new age merely because the cache write happened later. If the result has already exceeded the hard age budget, the model discards it.</p>
<p>A real implementation needs explicit time and source-version assumptions, as the expiration and invalidation articles explain. Its Redis TTL should not remove the value at the soft threshold if the application expects to serve it afterwards. Eviction can still remove it early, so stale-while-revalidate cannot eliminate the ordinary miss path.</p>
<p>Some systems permit additional staleness during errors, often called <strong>stale-if-error</strong>. That is another policy decision with another bound, not an excuse to extend deadlines whenever refresh fails. It may suit a public description while being unacceptable for permissions, account state or a consequential purchase decision.</p>

<h2>Early refresh and warming need a work budget</h2>
<p>A popular key may be worth refreshing before its soft threshold. A cold deployment may benefit from warming a small set of frequently used entries. Both approaches move work earlier; neither makes it free.</p>
<p>Refresh work should share the same per-key coordination as demand-driven loads. Otherwise a scheduled refresh, a background refresh and a user miss can all duplicate the same query. Cap total refresh concurrency and queue size, and stop low-value work from crowding out user requests.</p>
<p>Warm from evidence about likely reuse, not by copying the entire database into Redis. A broad warm-up can create the very source overload the cache was meant to prevent. It can also evict useful entries and align their new expiry times. Spread the work and measure how much of the warmed data is actually requested.</p>
<p>A sparse key may receive no request during its stale-while-revalidate window. In that case no demand-triggered refresh starts, and the next request after the hard limit must take the miss path. Background scheduling can change this behaviour, at the cost of loading values nobody may use.</p>

<h2>Duplicate suppression is not aggregate admission control</h2>
<p>One source load per key sounds small until ten thousand different keys go cold together. A perfect per-key coalescer can still admit ten thousand source operations. Limiting duplication and limiting total source work are different responsibilities.</p>
<p>Test the cache with a genuinely empty dataset, expired hot keys, many distinct misses, a slow loader, owner death and failed refreshes. Record source attempts, active loads, queued callers, waiting time and the age of served answers. A healthy average hit rate can hide a short burst that exceeds the source’s capacity.</p>
<p>The design is a composition: coalesce equivalent requests; spread correlated deadlines; use leases only within their ownership and failure limits; serve old answers only within an explicit policy; and bound all the work that reaches the source. None of these measures excuses the others.</p>
